<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="ws_4_4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>4.4 Practice Worksheet</title>

<exercisegroup>
<title>Power Functions</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Identify the exponent and the coefficient of each power function.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=\dfrac{7}{2x^3}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We have <m>y=\frac{7}{2}x^{-3}</m> so the coefficient is <m>\frac{7}{2}</m> and the exponent is <m>-3</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=6\sqrt[3]{x}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We have <m>y=6 x^{1 /3}</m> so the coefficient is <m>6</m> and the exponent is <m>1 /3</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=\dfrac{25x^4}{125x^7}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Note that <m>\frac{25}{125}</m> reduces to <m>\frac{1}{5}</m>. Therefore
<md>
y \quad = \quad \frac{1}{5} x^{4-7} \quad = \quad \frac{1}{5}x^{-3}.
</md> So the coefficient is <m>\frac{1}{5}</m> and the exponent is <m>-3</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=\dfrac{2x^4}{3\sqrt{x}}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
y \quad = \quad \frac{2x^{4}}{3x^{1 /2}}  \quad = \quad \frac{2}{3}x^{4 - 1/2} \quad = \quad \frac{2}{3}x^{7 /2}.
</md> So the coefficient is <m>\frac{2}{3}</m> and the exponent is <m>\frac{7}{2}</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=(5x^2)^{-2}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Here we have <m>y=(5)^{-2} \cdot (x^2)^{-2} = \frac{1}{5^2} \cdot x^{-4} = \frac{1}{25}x^{-4}</m>. So the coefficient is <m>\frac{1}{25}</m> and the exponent is <m>-4</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=(0.5x^{-3})^2(5x^6)^3</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>y \amp \quad = \quad (0.5)^2 (x^{-3})^2 (5)^3 (x^6)^3 </mrow>
<mrow> \amp \quad = \quad \frac{1}{4} \cdot x^{-6} \cdot 125 \cdot x^{18}</mrow>
<mrow> \amp \quad = \quad \frac{125}{4} \cdot x^{12}.</mrow>
</md> Therefore the coefficient is <m>\frac{125}{4}</m> and the exponent is <m>12</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


</exercisegroup>



<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
Draw a rough sketch of the graph of a power function <m>y=kx^p</m> with the stated properties.
</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
negative coefficient and an exponent of <m>\frac{5}{4}</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<image width="50%" xml:id="ws_4_4_1">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="ws_4_4_1_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\begin{axis}[grid=none,
axis lines=middle,
xmin=-2,xmax=2,
ymin=-2,ymax=2,
xtick=\empty,
ytick=\empty,
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
samples=250]
\addplot[domain=0:2 ,thick] {-x^1.2};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
negative coefficient and an exponent of <m>-3</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>

<image width="50%" xml:id="ws_4_4_2">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="ws_4_4_2_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\begin{axis}[grid=none,
axis lines=middle,
xmin=-3,xmax=3,
ymin=-3,ymax=3,
xtick=\empty,
ytick=\empty,
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
samples=250]
\addplot[domain=-3:-0.1 ,thick] {-1/x};
\addplot[domain=0.1:3 ,thick] {-1/x};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</solution>
</task>


